    // sampling collisions at exactly one viewport (1440x900 desktop).
    // That fixed band was wrong everywhere else: on a phone, the real
    // gap between the tagline and the quote is much taller than on
    // desktop, so the same fraction-of-H math left a large dead zone
    // of empty black between them and squeezed the wave down to a
    // flat, minor squiggle instead of the dominant, hypnotic curve the
    // reference prototype has -- a friend's side-by-side screenshot
    // caught this precisely (their reference vs. this site on the same
    // phone). Measuring the ACTUAL rendered gap between the real
    // elements instead, so the channel -- and the wave that fills it --
    // adapts to whatever room genuinely exists on that viewport, phone
    // or desktop alike, rather than a number tuned for one screen.
    // nerveMin/nerveMax are the RENDERED channel bounds -- what
    // nerveBaseY and every fragment/specimen placement actually reads.
    // They start flat and pinned near the top of a still-black screen
    // on purpose (see resize()'s entrance setup below) and ease toward
    // realNerveMin/realNerveMax -- the measured target -- once that's
    // known. This used to be a single pair of variables computed
    // directly by computeNerveChannel, which meant its first (correctly
    // rejected, see that function's own comment) measurement left the
    // channel at whatever H*0.16/H*0.86 happened to equal before
    // resize() had ever run (0, since H was still 0) -- a real bug, a
    // flat line pinned to literal y=0 until the next successful
    // measurement snapped it into place. Splitting "rendered" from
    // "target" turns that snap into a deliberate, visible entrance.
    let nerveMin = 0, nerveMax = 0;
    let realNerveMin = 0, realNerveMax = 0;
    let haveRealChannel = false;
    let entranceInited = false;
    let entranceSettled = false;
    // Fixed-duration eased tween, not a continuous chase -- an
    // exponential approach (the approachFactor pattern used everywhere
    // else in this file) moves fastest at the very start, the instant
    // the target becomes known, which reads as an abrupt jolt rather
    // than an arrival. A real ease-in-out (slow, then faster through
    // the middle, then slow again into place) is what "elegant" here
    // actually looks like -- the same reasoning as tokens.css's own
    // --ease-slow, used for exactly this kind of unhurried
    // repositioning elsewhere on the site.
    let entranceStartT = -1;
    let entranceFromMin = 0, entranceFromMax = 0;
    const ENTRANCE_MS = 3000;
    function computeNerveChannel() {
      if (document.body.dataset.landing === 'field') {
